1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
An n8n workflow can finish successfully
and still fail the user.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,703
A slow call, a missing field, or an unhandled node error
can disappear into execution history.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,000
Pisama closes that gap.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,367
It captures, detects, asks for a human verdict,
freezes the label, and scores the next build.

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:31,800
This demo uses five synthetic teaching cases:
two clean and three failed.

6
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,700
They are never mixed into the release claim.

7
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:40,931
The generator shows each case identity
and its independently declared expected modes.

8
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,300
Now I run the workflow in n8n.

9
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:51,244
The upper branch evaluates every trace.
The lower branch retains a stable case identity.

10
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:58,000
Clean cases have empty failure-mode sets.
The timeout is labeled F13.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,700
The missing-field case fires contract and expression modes.
The node error is caught separately.

12
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:09,300
Expected and actual sets match.

13
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:15,932
On this repeat run, Pisama returns the same execution IDs
and marks every record deduplicated.

14
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:23,800
The workflow hands off to Pisama.
The detections list separates the failure classes.

15
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:27,800
I open the inventory timeout.

16
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:35,700
The finding keeps the measured 64-second duration,
both thresholds, and the Slow Code node.

17
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:40,300
The execution trace supports F13.

18
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,249
I can accept it, reject it, or mark it fixed.
I mark it useful.

19
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:51,500
The missing-field case tells a different story.

20
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:58,587
Pisama identifies the contract break and failing expression,
so the reviewer sees cause and evidence.

21
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:08,698
These five synthetic cases teach the flow.
They stay outside the verified corpus.

22
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,500
Evaluation is the release gate.

23
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:22,800
Nineteen provenance-backed cases are reviewed and frozen.
A normal run scores eighteen regression cases.

24
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:26,700
The protected holdout remains excluded.

25
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,000
I start a new asynchronous run.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,324
It succeeds at 100 percent exact-set accuracy,
stored as an immutable record.

27
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:40,500
If a label changes, Pisama appends a revision
with new evidence.

28
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:46,367
It never rewrites label history or earlier score runs.
That is what makes the loop auditable.

29
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:52,500
From one failed n8n run to a reviewed,
reproducible release signal.

30
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:55,154
That is the Pisama closed loop.
